<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of guidance scale <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on gender parity.<span id="S4.T1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The best values are highlighted in bold for each metric and type of guidance. Here, <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <em id="S4.T1.7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Male</em> attribute. The lowest FD is achieved for our Jeffrey guidance at <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>. For standard guidance, it is achieved at <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=3.0</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that, for our method, this improvement does not come at the expense of FID.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:138.0pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2606.13240v1/grid_baseline_balance_6.png" id="S4.SS2.SSS1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/34;" width="191" height="34" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2606.13240v1/grid_guidance_jeffrey_balance_6.png" id="S4.SS2.SSS1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/39;" width="191" height="39" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2606.13240v1/grid_guidance_standard_balance_6.png" id="S4.SS2.SSS1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/40;" width="191" height="40" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of guidance for gender parity.<span id="S4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Jeffrey guidance selectively modifies samples (highlighted in red) leaving the most confident samples essentially unchanged, while standard guidance modifies a lot more samples. More samples are available in <a href="#A5.F14" title="In Appendix E Attribute guidance for fairness: additional figures ‣ Towards More General Control of Diffusion Models Using Jeffrey Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show as a proof of concept that Jeffrey guidance allows to steer the generated samples toward a balanced distribution of the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Male</span> attribute (which is binary), represented through the feature <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a careful choice of guidance strength.
Following previous works on fairness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parihar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tiwary et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we evaluate distribution matching using the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fairness Discrepancy</span> (FD). This metric measures the deviation between a target distribution <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and the average classifier predictions over generated samples: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FD}=\left\|\bar{p}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})}\big[c_{\psi}(\mathbf{x})\big]\right\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>FD</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FD}=\left\|\bar{p}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})}\big[c_{\psi}(\mathbf{x})\big]\right\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the target attribute distribution and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\psi}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\psi}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> the softmax outputs of a pre-trained attribute classifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As measured by the FD in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2.1 Distribution matching for gender parity ‣ 4.2 Attribute distribution matching and decorrelation with Jeffrey guidance for fairness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Towards More General Control of Diffusion Models Using Jeffrey Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, moderate guidance strengths allow us to closely match the target distribution. In particular, using <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, the proportion of males shifts from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.296" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.296</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.296</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.522" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.522</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.522</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing the FD from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.288</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.032" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.032</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.032</annotation></semantics></math>. Larger guidance strengths eventually overcompensate and bias the distribution in the opposite direction, reaching a male proportion of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.889" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.889</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.889</annotation></semantics></math>. Interestingly, achieving near-perfect balance does not translate into a significant degradation of FID, although excessively strong guidance increases it to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 35.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>35.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 35.08</annotation></semantics></math>. This highlights a similar trade-off to standard class-conditional guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2022</a>)</cite>, where the guidance strength must be chosen to balance sample quality and faithfulness to the desired target distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2.1 Distribution matching for gender parity ‣ 4.2 Attribute distribution matching and decorrelation with Jeffrey guidance for fairness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Towards More General Control of Diffusion Models Using Jeffrey Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> also shows the benefit of Jeffrey guidance over the natural baseline combining ancestral sampling with standard guidance (i.e., drawing the attribute class randomly and then guiding towards the drawn class). While simple class-conditional guidance does achieve a balanced distribution with FD <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.014</annotation></semantics></math>, it comes at the cost of higher FID valued to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 24.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>24.33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 24.33</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2.1 Distribution matching for gender parity ‣ 4.2 Attribute distribution matching and decorrelation with Jeffrey guidance for fairness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Towards More General Control of Diffusion Models Using Jeffrey Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> suggests the baseline approach leads to changing more samples’ attribute compared to Jeffrey guidance. We believe this is because Jeffrey guidance continuously balances both class directions along the sampling trajectory (see <a href="#A1.E19" title="In Appendix A Attribute guidance: deriving the guidance terms with a classifier ‣ Towards More General Control of Diffusion Models Using Jeffrey Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), whereas standard guidance pushes each sample entirely toward a single class.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Decorrelation of Young and Male</h4>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of guidance scale <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on correlation <math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="S4.T2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <em id="S4.T2.7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Male</em> and <em id="S4.T2.7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Young</em> attributes, respectively. The very low correlation achieved (at <math id="S4.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=3</annotation></semantics></math>, in bold) comes with relatively minor changes in the marginal distributions and a limited degradation of about one FID point. Notably, the most pronounced shift in the joint distribution is a reduction in the proportion of samples that are both female and young, which constitutes the dominant group in the unguided setting.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:375.0pt;height:66.2pt;vertical-align:-31.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-20.8pt,3.7pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Marginals</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4"><math id="S4.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}},\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}},\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Guidance <math id="S4.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S4.T2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}},\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}},\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}{=}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{m}}{=}1)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}}{=}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathbf{y}_{\mathrm{y}}{=}1)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">FID <math id="S4.T2.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\downarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\downarrow)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.8.1.3" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;" alttext="0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;" mathbackground="#E6E6FF" mathsize="0.900em">0.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0</annotation></semantics></math></th>
